-# ntsco
-ntsc
+# ntsc_ — Student Toolkit
+
+A responsive student portal for classes, courses, messages, attendance, exams, results, study content, chemistry practice, rank prediction and administrative utilities.
+
+## Run locally
+
+Requires Node.js 20+.
+
+```sh
+npm ci
+npm run dev          # http://localhost:3000, binds 0.0.0.0
+npm run check        # syntax, links, original IDs and handler contracts
+npm run build        # deployment-ready static files in dist/
+npx playwright install chromium
+npm test             # isolated browser regressions; never calls production APIs
+```
+
+`PORT=3001 npm run dev` changes the port. Source changes rebuild the compatibility bundles automatically; refresh your browser to see changes. For an existing Chromium installation, set `CHROMIUM_PATH=/path/to/chromium` when running tests.
+
+## Architecture
+
+```text
+src/
+  core/              Configuration/state, session helpers, formatters, API adapters
+  features/          Domain behavior: exams, resources, messages, attendance,
+                     chemistry, reagents, pKa, neural tools, navigation, auth,
+                     caching/data orchestration, bootstrap and recordings
+  shell/             Native ES modules: auth presentation, accessible navigation,
+                     mobile search, dialog focus and keyboard interactions
+  shared/            Weekday-first agenda, inspector frames and topic ledgers
+  styles/
+    tokens.css       Semantic light/dark palettes, accent presets, local fonts
+    login.css        Console-style authentication
+    workspace.css    Application shell and shared components
+    responsive.css   Desktop, tablet and mobile adaptations
+    pages.css        Populated records and detail views: courses, results,
+                     schedules, chemistry, dialogs and mobile dock
+    console-pages.css New paper index, score instruments, ranking ledger
+                     and subject-coloured session timelines
+    schedule.css     Prominent weekday headings and standalone batch agenda
+    settings.css     Settings control surface and recording-player dialog
+    inspectors.css   Syllabus, exam/course information, attendance and result dialogs
+    neural-console.css Inference inputs/results, reconstruction and Functions directory
+    tools/           Shared standalone shell, workbenches and loaded records
+    legacy/          Preserved feature styles, grouped by domain
+functions/           Seven independently-addressable utility pages
+assets/              Toolkit identity and licensed, self-hosted fonts
+scripts/
+  manifest.json      Explicit, deterministic source order
+  build.mjs          Dependency-free compatibility bundle + static-site build
+  dev.mjs            Local server and source watcher
+  check.mjs          Structural and JavaScript syntax checks
+```
+
+### Source of truth and compatibility boundary
+
+Edit **`src/`**, not root `main.js`, `core.js`, `styles.css`, or `tools.css`: these files are generated by `npm run build`. They remain checked in so the existing static deployment and standalone utility URLs continue working without requiring a runtime server or framework.
+
+The old monolithic application has been split by responsibility. Its business-logic sources are assembled in their original order into classic scripts, intentionally preserving the global handlers and shared state expected by existing HTML and utility pages. New shell functionality uses ES modules and communicates through `data-navigate` controls and the `portal:navigate` event. This is an incremental architecture migration, not an incompatible rewrite of the API layer. Feature markup and legacy global state remain compatibility boundaries for a future component-by-component migration.
+
+The stylesheet build preserves domain styles first, then applies the new design system, shell and responsive layer. This avoids removing specialized exam, chemistry, recording or leaderboard layouts while giving the portal a consistent new appearance.
+
+### Design and behavior
+
+- Charcoal/lime workbench UI, matching light mode and retained accent presets.
+- Today's class schedule comes first. Extra utilities live in the directory rather than promotional dashboard cards.
+- Course records use compact artwork, explicit dates and working detail controls. No fabricated progress bars.
+- Exams and Results use aligned paper indexes, not legacy exam cards. Results uses the existing forced-result API flow, including manual test-ID lookup and unpublished papers. Past unpublished papers are labelled Awaiting result, not Live.
+- Result analysis uses a score/max gauge, comparison bars, rank instruments and subject ledgers. Null scores remain unavailable, zero/negative scores are retained, and raw data and additional tools stay collapsible. PDF, OMR, answer-key, leaderboard and solutions actions are preserved.
+- Subject-coloured session timelines keep the schedule first on Overview and expand into a multi-day agenda in Classes. Large THU/TUE-style weekday labels lead; dates are secondary. The batch scheduler shares this presentation, and recorded-class rows are weekday-led too.
+- Settings → Functions navigates to the single `tools` page. There is no second Settings tool grid or expanding legacy panel. Category/search filters, all seven utility links and the two ID launchers live in that directory. Settings retains explicit data-operation scopes. Refresh exam data actually reloads exam data; cache clearing preserves appearance, practice progress and other accounts’ timetable caches. Cloud sync exposes success/failure without claiming a successful sync on errors.
+- Quick launchers retain test access checks and open the real recording player. The player is a body-level accessible dialog, not a child of a hidden practice sheet. The list editor can retry after restricted access or loading errors.
+- The seven standalone tools share chrome and navigation, with dedicated layouts for registry metadata/downloads, exam taking, export jobs, schedules and recordings.
+- Mobile content reserves space for the four-item navigation dock: **Overview · Results · Classes · Settings**. Drawers and detail dialogs support keyboard dismissal and focus management.
+- Chemistry home, collection, learn and practice views share the same controls; the pKa collection exposes its existing add/remove functionality.
+- Neural tools use new input/output console markup, expandable normalization traces, anchor records, inline validation, and separate empty/result states. Model dependency failures can be retried without retaining a failed script. Inference/generation algorithms and export/filter controls remain connected.
+- Syllabus and calendar details share a subject-coloured topic index with the standalone schedule explorer. Course, attendance and result dialogs use the same inspector frame; the legacy modal card markup is no longer rendered.
+- Self-hosted fonts, reduced-motion support and on-demand TensorFlow loading.
+
+## Deployment
+
+Run `npm run build` and publish **`dist/`** to any static HTTPS host. CSS and script URLs are content-versioned automatically so old cached styling is not paired with new HTML. The included Cloudflare `_headers` file requests revalidation. No SPA rewrite rule is required; the existing `functions/*.html` URLs remain real pages. `dist/` contains only application assets, not tests, source tooling, or repository metadata.
+
+## Verification and integrations
+
+The browser suite includes login/navigation, feature access checks, themes, responsive shell geometry, all seven standalone tools and populated main-page fixtures. It exercises course details, result/leaderboard/solution drill-downs, solution-image loading, calendar/syllabus dialogs, message threads, pKa collection/learn/practice, virtualized resource rows and mobile registry stats. Detail regressions cover clipboard output, dialog focus, neural validation/inference with mocked model outputs, anchor editing, mode selection, reconstruction pagination/filtering, CSV export and dependency retry. Settings regressions click through the Functions route and all seven tool links, verify appearance/preferences, cache clearing/reload, refresh and sync feedback, practice collection controls, and the exam/recording quick launchers. Console regressions cover the exact dock destinations, forced-result data flow, pending-paper statuses, safe ID handling, score edge cases and subject-coloured multi-day schedules. `tests/fixtures/` is test-only and is never included in the production build.
+
+`tests/feature-contract.json` records 250 retained DOM IDs and 255 named functions; `tests/tool-contract.json` preserves standalone IDs. The checker also verifies content-versioned asset references.
+
+Tests intentionally block external traffic and do not read, submit, or modify real student data. Live login, account-specific results, submissions, downloads, recordings, cloud sync and model predictions still require verification with an authorized account and working upstream services.
+
+Existing external integrations (Narayana APIs, the Cloudflare login/proxy worker, Supabase, chemistry drawing libraries and rank model assets) retain their endpoints, access checks and data formats. This UI/architecture work does not change the existing backend security or credential-persistence policy. Review that policy separately before a security-sensitive deployment.
